From: rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu (Ray)
Date: Mon, 26 Sep 94 5:03:19 WET DST
Subject: [#94-9-506] copyright of the future

   Copyright isn't unhip. Copyright *law* might be. But what could
be more extropian than a freely signed license agreement or
usage contract that is enforced by arbitration in private court,
or future ostracism?

   Personally, I believe the future of cash in software (or copyright)
is in distributed information and software. Superdistribution of
software and information will prevent pirating because the value
is in the dynamic form of the software, not the static
"captured form"  Remote software objects on a distributed  
network can hide their implementation so that you must pay
for the service they provide.  Distributed hypertext documents
will make their money from the database server access fees.

    Finally, check the cypherpunk archives for an article I posted
about 2 months ago about cryptographically strong p-code interpreters
and how they can be used to stop software cracking. Consider a program
flow diagram to be a graph. This graph can be hidden easily by adding
lots of other useless edges. Now disassembling the execution of the
program (without running it) is about as hard as finding the
hamiltonian in a graph. Theoretically, a cracker could still defeat
the protection, but in practice, it would stop most piracy. An
obfuscating compiler can achieve this goal without much of a speed
penalty and given that processors are getting faster and faster,
software publishers might accept it (just as developers are
moving to higher level, and safer, languages. Object
orientation, garbage collection, run time type checking, array
bounds checking, incremental compilation, etc. These slow
down code, but improve production and maintainence costs.
Hell, Visual Basic is being used to develop a lot of applications
today.)

-Ray




