Subject: [#94-9-507] copyright of the future

Yet another attempt at "tagging":
> They do a "diff" of their copies and discover that the 100'th word
> is sometimes "but" and sometimes "however", and similarly for 20
> other words throughout the document.

The "information liberators" (as long as I'm describe their
strategy I might as well also demonstrate their PR :-) can insert 
a third synonym for each differing word, thereby eliminating half 
the fingerprint for each distinctly tagged copy available to be 
compared.  The number of words needed to be flipped is thus exponential 
with the number of copies available to the liberators. Thus, for example, 
comparing two 20-word binary tags would reduce its its uniqueness to 10
bits, or 1 in 1024 copies.  Comparing a third fingerprint would 
reduce uniqueness to 5 bits, or 1 in 32 copies.  In general
if 2^(N/2^C) copies are distributed with N bit fingerprints,
the liberators need obtain only only C copies to lose the fingerprint.
In other words out of D copies distributed the liberators need
to obtain only log(N/log(D)) copies.  

If you pick words that only have two good synonyms, the liberators
can simply search for all instances of such words, whether they are 
found to differ or not, and randomly flip most of them to the other 
synonym. The content damage thereby induced will not, on average, 
be much greater than that induced by the original synonym flipping 
process.

In general, if the liberators know the algorithm used, they can simply
rerun it on their copies, changing the same kinds of words that the
algorithm would have changed.  If the algorithm randomly
changes only 1 in N good synonym candidates, liberators can simply
change N/2 of the good candidates: a quantitative, but not
a qualitative amount of additional degradation in the text.
Changing only N/2 of the candidates would be as good as getting
another copy to compare.  Given two copies, all N good canddiates
can be XORd, obliterating the fingerprint.  If the same synonym
pair is used more than once in the same document, transposition
can be used to scramble that part of the fingerprint.

As usual with these schemes a thief can simply steal the
copy of an innocent third party, and the fingerprint points
at the third party.  Or a thief could sell his own copy to
a pirate, and plausibly claim it was stolen.  Finally, 
customers would have to be securely identified in order for
the fingerprint to actually point to the contracting agent
for punishment of copyright infringement; this would shut
out customers who desire privacy from dossier collection
(like me).

