From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Date: Mon, 26 Sep 1994 04:57:11 -0700 (PDT)
Subject: [#94-9-508] Cryonics and Metabolic Dependent State Loss

Doug Cutrell:
> It's not clear to me how low the body temperature
> would have to drop before we could be sure that all energy transport
> mechanisms had ceased. 

The energy transport is at least greatly slowed down, so that 
the storage mechanism must be robust enough to survive at several
different orders of magnitude rate of metabolism.  Any 
requirement for robustness is a major strike against
quantum coherence as a storage mechanism, since it is fragile
to event the tiniest of changes in its substrate or nearby environment. 
I find quantum coherence as a brain storage mechanism extremely 
improbable even for metabolically active short-term storage due to its 
high instability.

The most extreme examples of naturally reversed metabolic slowdown are 
probably a few known species of frogs and fish that can be frozen for 
several months, thaw, and recover in the wild.  They don't freeze solid, 
as cells are mostly dehydrated, the water replaced by glucose and antifreeze
proteins.  Nevertheless, these vertebrate nervous systems can sit 
far below normal freezing point over several months and fully recover. 
Thus no kind of long term storage important to two very different
kinds of vertebrates requires temperatures or metabolic activity above 
that at freezing point.

